Here's an Apps Script function that reads a name from cell A1 and writes a greeting in cell B1:

```javascript
function writeGreeting() {
  // Get the active spreadsheet and the active sheet
  var sheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSpreadsheet().getActiveSheet();

  // Read the name from cell A1
  var name = sheet.getRange("A1").getValue();

  // Build the greeting message
  var greeting = "Hello, " + name + "!";

  // Write the greeting to cell B1
  sheet.getRange("B1").setValue(greeting);
}
```

### How to use it:

1. Open your Google Sheet.
2. Go to **Extensions > Apps Script**.
3. Delete any boilerplate code and paste the function above.
4. Save the project (e.g., name it "GreetingScript").
5. Put a name in cell **A1** (e.g., `Ava`).
6. Run the `writeGreeting` function:
   - Click the **Run** button (▶) in the toolbar, or
   - Select `writeGreeting` from the function dropdown and click **Run**.
7. Grant any permissions requested (first run only).
8. Check cell **B1** — it should now show `Hello, Ava!`

### Optional: Auto-run on edit

If you want the greeting to update automatically whenever A1 changes, use an `onEdit` trigger instead:

```javascript
function onEdit(e) {
  var sheet = e.source.getActiveSheet();
  var editedCell = e.range;

  // Only run if the edited cell is A1
  if (editedCell.getA1Notation() === "A1") {
    var name = editedCell.getValue();
    var greeting = "Hello, " + name + "!";
    sheet.getRange("B1").setValue(greeting);
  }
}
```

This special function name (`onEdit`) is automatically triggered whenever a user edits the spreadsheet — no manual running required. Just paste this instead of (or alongside) the first function, and it will fire every time you type something into A1.
